└── synchronous HTTP client for polling a REST gateway
    ├── Candidates : requests, httpx, aiohttp
    ├── Used APIs  : Session, session.get, resp.raise_for_status, resp.json
    ├── Pitfalls   : requests.Session() vs httpx.Client(): timeout semantics differ 
    │   (httpx requires explicit timeout objects/defaults vs requests' simpler float 
    │   args); Exception types differ: requests.exceptions.HTTPError vs 
    │   httpx.HTTPStatusError; aiohttp requires ClientSession as an async context 
    │   manager and all call sites (read_tag, poll) to be rewritten with async/await; 
    │   aiohttp has no synchronous .get() equivalent; session.get() must be awaited 
    │   inside 'async with'; resp.raise_for_status() exists in both requests and httpx 
    │   but behaves slightly differently in edge cases (redirects, streaming)
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── The file uses requests.Session() to perform blocking GET requests, check status 
        codes, and parse JSON. httpx offers a nearly identical synchronous Client API 
        (Client().get(), raise_for_status(), .json()) making it a plausible drop-in with
        some glue code. aiohttp could theoretically serve the same HTTP-fetch role but 
        is async-first, requiring the polling methods (read_tag/poll) to become async 
        coroutines -- a deeper architectural change than a simple import swap.

