└── Modbus TCP client for reading holding registers from a power meter
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, minimalmodbus, modbus-tk
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, client.connect, client.read_holding_registers, 
    │   result.isError, result.registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : minimalmodbus is primarily designed for serial/RTU communication; 
    │   TCP support is less idiomatic and requires different connection setup 
    │   (Instrument class vs ModbusTcpClient); minimalmodbus raises exceptions on read 
    │   errors instead of returning a response object with .isError(); minimalmodbus's 
    │   read_registers returns a raw list of ints, not an object with a .registers 
    │   attribute; modbus-tk uses a different master/query API (modbus_tcp.TcpMaster, 
    │   execute()) requiring rewritten call sites; unit/slave id handling differs across
    │   libraries (unit= kwarg vs slave address positional args); error handling model 
    │   changes from isError()-check to try/except across all alternatives
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── pymodbus is used here strictly as a Modbus TCP master to read holding registers,
        returning a response object with .isError()/.registers. minimalmodbus and 
        modbus-tk both implement Modbus master functionality (TCP/RTU) and could serve 
        the same functional role of communicating with a Modbus meter, though their APIs
        differ substantially. The file's own docstring explicitly calls out 
        pymodbus->minimalmodbus as the intended substitution target, confirming this is 
        a genuine equivalence class for the 'read modbus registers' role.

