    │   object (model=self); fysom has similar but not identical callback/argument 
    │   conventions; python-statemachine requires defining States/Transitions as class 
    │   attributes via subclassing, incompatible with the runtime Machine(model=self, 
    │   states=..., initial=...) construction used here; automat uses a 
    │   MethodicalMachine decorator-based declarative style with 
    │   @machine.state()/@machine.input() decorators, requiring a full rewrite of the 
    │   class structure; wildcard transitions ('*' -> 'FAULT') syntax is 
    │   transitions-specific and must be manually re-implemented in alternatives; 
    │   conditions='can_fill' / after='open_inlet' string-based callback resolution 
    │   differs across libraries and may need explicit callable references
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── The `transitions` library is used to build a dynamic state machine bound to the 
        controller instance (model=self), auto-generating trigger methods (start_fill, 
        trip, etc.) and exposing self.state. `fysom` offers a very similar imperative 
        API (add_transition-style callbacks, dynamic state/event binding) and is a near 
        drop-in for this usage pattern. `python-statemachine` and `automat` are also FSM
        libraries covering the same functional role (declarative state/transition 
        modeling) but use fundamentally different class-based/decorator-based APIs 
        rather than the model-binding pattern used here.

