└── outbound email/notification delivery
    ├── Candidates : smtplib, apprise
    ├── Used APIs  : smtplib.SMTP, SMTP.sendmail
    ├── Pitfalls   : smtplib.sendmail(sender, recipients, raw_message) is a low-level, 
    │   synchronous, connection-oriented API; apprise.notify(title=..., body=...) is a 
    │   fire-and-forget, config-URL-driven API with a completely different signature; 
    │   apprise does not expose raw MIME message construction the way this code builds 
    │   it manually (From/To/Subject headers) — that logic would need to be reworked or 
    │   dropped; apprise requires service URLs (e.g. mailto://) configured ahead of time
    │   rather than a live SMTP connection object being dependency-injected; No built-in
    │   equivalent for the SMTP object substitution pattern used here for testability 
    │   (smtp=None default) — apprise's AppriseAsset/Apprise objects would need similar 
    │   DI wrapping; smtplib as used here has no TLS/STARTTLS — plaintext SMTP; a 
    │   migration should also consider adding encryption regardless of backend
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── The file's own docstring identifies apprise as the intended substitution target 
        for smtplib: both can be used to deliver an alarm notification (smtplib sends 
        raw SMTP email; apprise can dispatch to email and dozens of other channels). 
        Although apprise wasn't surfaced by the discovery step, it's explicitly named 
        in-file as the swap target, so it merits inclusion as a plausible (if 
        non-trivial) alternative for the 'notify operator of alarm' role.

