Equivalence Groups Found
└── Modbus TCP client for industrial field I/O (register read/write)
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, modbus-tk
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, client.connect, client.read_holding_registers, 
    │   client.write_register, rr.isError, rr.registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : Different response objects: pymodbus uses 
    │   rr.isError()/rr.registers, pyModbusTCP typically returns None on failure and a 
    │   list of registers on success, modbus-tk raises exceptions instead of returning 
    │   error objects; Error handling model differs significantly (exception-based vs. 
    │   sentinel/return-value-based) requiring rewrites of the error-checking logic in 
    │   _read1 and write_valve; Unit/slave ID parameter naming and passing conventions 
    │   differ between libraries (unit= vs slave_id= vs positional); Connection setup 
    │   and lifecycle management (connect/close, context managers) differ across 
    │   libraries
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All three libraries provide Modbus TCP master/client functionality capable of 
        reading and writing holding registers over TCP/IP, matching the ModbusTcpClient 
        usage in this file. pyModbusTCP is a lightweight TCP-only Modbus client with a 
        similar synchronous API, and modbus-tk supports both TCP and RTU with a 
        comparable request/response model. minimalmodbus was excluded because it is 
        designed primarily for serial (RS-232/RS-485) RTU communication and lacks 
        first-class TCP client support, making it a poor functional match for this 
        file's TCP-based usage.

