Equivalence Groups Found
└── synchronous HTTP client (session-based requests to REST endpoints)
    ├── Candidates : requests, httpx
    ├── Used APIs  : Session, session.get, session.post, raise_for_status, json, 
    │   status_code
    ├── Pitfalls   : httpx.Client vs requests.Session: constructor and connection 
    │   pooling defaults differ slightly; httpx raises httpx.HTTPStatusError vs 
    │   requests.HTTPError on raise_for_status(); httpx timeout object (httpx.Timeout) 
    │   differs from requests' float/tuple timeout semantics for connect vs read 
    │   timeouts; httpx does not automatically follow redirects by default (must set 
    │   follow_redirects=True), unlike requests
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── The code uses requests.Session() to perform GET/POST calls with timeout, 
        raise_for_status(), and .json() parsing. httpx.Client exposes a near-identical 
        sync API (get/post, raise_for_status, .json()) and can be used as a drop-in 
        replacement for the session object, matching the file's own comment about 
        substitution intent. aiohttp is rejected because it is async-only and would 
        require rewriting the entire ScadaClient to async/await, which is a 
        fundamentally different calling convention not achievable via a simple 
        import/call-site swap. urllib3 is rejected because requests is built on top of 
        urllib3 (dependency chain), and urllib3's lower-level API (PoolManager, no 
        built-in JSON handling, no raise_for_status) does not match the high-level 
        session semantics used here.

