requests: aiohttp
Equivalence Groups Found
└── synchronous HTTP client (session-based requests with get/post/json/status handling)
    ├── Candidates : requests, httpx, urllib3
    ├── Used APIs  : Session, session.get, session.post, r.raise_for_status, r.json, 
    │   r.status_code
    ├── Pitfalls   : httpx.Client vs requests.Session: parameter names and default 
    │   timeout behavior differ (httpx defaults to enabling timeouts, requests defaults 
    │   to none); httpx raises httpx.HTTPStatusError vs requests.HTTPError -- exception 
    │   handling code must be updated; httpx response object API is similar but not 
    │   identical (e.g., .json(), .raise_for_status() exist but streaming and cookie 
    │   handling differ); urllib3 has a much lower-level API; there is no direct 
    │   Session/response.json()/raise_for_status() equivalent, requiring significant 
    │   glue code; connection pooling and retry configuration differ significantly 
    │   between libraries
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── The file uses requests.Session() to perform GET/POST calls with timeout, JSON 
        payloads, raise_for_status(), and .json() parsing. httpx offers a near drop-in 
        sync Client with matching method names (get/post/json/raise_for_status), making 
        it a viable substitute if used in sync mode. urllib3 (PoolManager) can also 
        perform HTTP requests but lacks the same high-level Session/response object API 
        (no built-in raise_for_status/json convenience in the same form), so it's a 
        weaker but still plausible low-level alternative. aiohttp is excluded since it 
        is async-only and would require rewriting the entire client to async/await, 
        which is a much deeper architectural change than a simple substitution.

