    ├── Pitfalls   : aiomqtt and gmqtt are async-first (asyncio-based), requiring the 
    │   entire connect/publish flow to become async, whereas paho-mqtt's 
    │   Client.connect/publish are synchronous; aiomqtt uses async context managers 
    │   (`async with Client(...) as client`) instead of explicit connect() calls; Return
    │   value shape differs: paho-mqtt's publish() returns a MQTTMessageInfo object with
    │   `.rc`, async clients typically don't return an rc code in the same way, 
    │   requiring adaptation of the `getattr(info, 'rc', 0)` logic; amqtt is a 
    │   broker+client library with a different API surface and is less actively 
    │   maintained; Threading/event-loop model differs significantly, impacting how 
    │   MqttSink would need to be instantiated and used by callers
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── paho-mqtt is used here as a synchronous, callback-based MQTT client to connect 
        and publish JSON payloads. aiomqtt, gmqtt, and amqtt are all MQTT client 
        libraries that could functionally replace paho-mqtt for publishing messages to a
        broker, and the file's own docstring explicitly calls out aiomqtt as a 
        substitution target.

